1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
You've saved 10 percent of a home's price.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,320
[curious] Do you buy now and pay mortgage
insurance,

3
00:00:06,373 --> 00:00:08,960
or keep renting until you reach 20
percent?

4
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:14,447
On the schedule alone, you can't even ask
to cancel it for about 8 years.

5
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:18,447
We replayed real US prices and rates month
by month,

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,847
so you'll see how long it took on paper,
typically and in slow cases.

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:28,073
On paper means the loan is 80 percent of
the home's value by a national price

8
00:00:28,153 --> 00:00:28,984
index.

9
00:00:28,985 --> 00:00:31,673
That's not the same as getting the
insurance removed.

10
00:00:31,793 --> 00:00:36,873
Removal on today's value, when you ask, is
the loan owner's rule;

11
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:41,593
for Fannie Mae loans, that's a waiting
period and a 75 percent bar.

12
00:00:41,793 --> 00:00:47,513
You'll also meet three illustrative buyers
who got very different answers.

13
00:00:47,673 --> 00:00:53,994
It's US only, history, not a forecast, and
it won't say whether to buy or wait.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,280
Private mortgage insurance, or PMI, is a
policy lenders usually require on a

15
00:01:03,323 --> 00:01:07,040
conventional loan with less than 20
percent down.

16
00:01:07,093 --> 00:01:10,080
It protects the lender if the loan goes
bad,

17
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,440
but the borrower pays for it, month after
month,

18
00:01:13,493 --> 00:01:15,040
on top of the mortgage.

19
00:01:15,136 --> 00:01:18,800
What it costs depends on the loan and the
borrower,

20
00:01:18,827 --> 00:01:21,280
so this video puts no dollar figure on it.

21
00:01:21,376 --> 00:01:24,160
What we can measure is how long it lasts.

22
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:26,160
So when does it end?

23
00:01:27,167 --> 00:01:30,687
Here's an illustrative example, in dollars
of the day:

24
00:01:30,727 --> 00:01:35,007
a $400,000 home, a little under the
national median price of new homes sold

25
00:01:35,039 --> 00:01:36,767
from April through June.

26
00:01:36,943 --> 00:01:42,287
With 10 percent down, that leaves a
$360,000 loan.

27
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:47,140
At September's average mortgage rate, 6.86
percent,

28
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:50,740
the monthly payment for principal and
interest is the figure on screen.

29
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:55,700
Taxes, home insurance and the mortgage
insurance all come on top of it.

30
00:01:55,748 --> 00:01:58,180
Each payment pays the balance down a
little,

31
00:01:58,237 --> 00:02:01,140
slowly at first, and faster later on.

32
00:02:02,133 --> 00:02:06,133
By federal law, the borrower can ask to
cancel the insurance once the schedule

33
00:02:06,277 --> 00:02:10,053
says the loan is down to 80 percent of the
original value,

34
00:02:10,197 --> 00:02:12,693
here $320,000.

35
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:17,733
On this loan, that's payment 99, the date
this video opened with.

36
00:02:17,957 --> 00:02:23,093
That request comes with conditions, such
as being current on payments.

37
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:29,220
And the insurance has to end automatically
when the schedule reaches 78 percent,

38
00:02:29,247 --> 00:02:30,660
as long as payments are current.

39
00:02:30,852 --> 00:02:34,260
Here, that's about 9.5 years in.

40
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:39,460
Both dates come from the schedule alone,
and both ignore what the house is worth.

41
00:02:40,467 --> 00:02:44,627
Lenders, and the investors who own loans,
can also drop the insurance based on the

42
00:02:44,675 --> 00:02:45,987
home's current value.

43
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:50,707
If prices rise, the same balance becomes a
smaller share of the home,

44
00:02:50,747 --> 00:02:53,507
and 80 percent can arrive sooner, on
paper.

45
00:02:53,619 --> 00:02:57,827
That's what on paper means in this video,
and a lender's request,

46
00:02:57,883 --> 00:03:00,707
appraisal and minimum time still come
after it.

47
00:03:00,894 --> 00:03:05,427
So how fast did rising prices actually get
10 percent down buyers there?

48
00:03:09,034 --> 00:03:15,513
We took every purchase month from January
1991 to July 2016.

49
00:03:15,733 --> 00:03:21,434
For each one, we set up the same 10
percent down loan at that month's average

50
00:03:21,513 --> 00:03:22,333
rate.

51
00:03:22,334 --> 00:03:27,353
And we let the home's value move exactly
like the Federal Housing Finance Agency's

52
00:03:27,452 --> 00:03:30,314
national index of home purchase prices.

53
00:03:30,447 --> 00:03:34,633
Every month after that, we compare the
balance with the value,

54
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:39,194
and stop the clock the first time the loan
is 80 percent or less.

55
00:03:39,418 --> 00:03:44,954
Each bar here is one purchase month, and
its height shows how long that took.

56
00:03:45,967 --> 00:03:49,007
The typical answer: 23 months.

57
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:51,807
Half of the purchase months got there
sooner,

58
00:03:51,847 --> 00:03:53,327
and half took longer.

59
00:03:53,527 --> 00:03:59,887
And in 90.6 percent of them, prices got
the loan to 80 percent on paper no later

60
00:03:59,919 --> 00:04:01,088
than the schedule would have.

61
00:04:01,439 --> 00:04:06,767
That's the gap between the schedule's long
road and the replay's typical one.

62
00:04:07,767 --> 00:04:08,967
But that's on paper.

63
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:15,927
We also checked a larger set: every
purchase month since January 1991 with two

64
00:04:15,994 --> 00:04:17,447
years of prices after it.

65
00:04:17,707 --> 00:04:24,407
Two years after purchase, only 15.6
percent of them were at or below the 75

66
00:04:24,457 --> 00:04:30,167
percent bar Fannie Mae sets for its loans
when a borrower asks to cancel on today's

67
00:04:30,234 --> 00:04:33,607
value, a separate route from the law's
schedule.

68
00:04:33,794 --> 00:04:39,047
So reaching 80 percent on paper in about
two years was common in this history,

69
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:42,167
while clearing that early bar by then was
not.

70
00:04:44,134 --> 00:04:47,174
The typical case also hides a long tail.

71
00:04:47,267 --> 00:04:51,334
About 1 in 7 purchase months took more
than 5 years.

72
00:04:51,462 --> 00:04:54,854
They sit together in one stretch of the
grid,

73
00:04:54,874 --> 00:04:58,214
the months just before and during the
national price slump.

74
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:04,640
The slowest was October 2005: 112 months
on paper.

75
00:05:04,768 --> 00:05:08,560
That's longer than the schedule itself
took at that month's rate.

76
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:12,640
And this is a national average: no single
home tracks it,

77
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:16,400
and a local market can do much better or
much worse.

78
00:05:16,693 --> 00:05:20,080
So why did the same rule give such
different answers?

79
00:05:21,667 --> 00:05:24,867
Three illustrative buyers, each with 10
percent down,

80
00:05:24,931 --> 00:05:25,931
show why.

81
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:31,347
Grace bought in June 2014, at 4.16
percent.

82
00:05:31,487 --> 00:05:35,827
Her loan reached 80 percent on paper right
at the middle of the replay:

83
00:05:35,907 --> 00:05:38,147
she is the typical case from before.

84
00:05:38,287 --> 00:05:41,187
Her own schedule would have taken several
years longer.

85
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:46,360
Owen bought in January 2004, while prices
were climbing fast.

86
00:05:46,547 --> 00:05:49,960
He reached 80 percent on paper in 13
months,

87
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:51,720
the fastest in the replay.

88
00:05:51,857 --> 00:05:55,560
Rising prices did most of that work, not
his payments.

89
00:05:56,567 --> 00:05:58,727
[serious] Victor bought the year after
Owen,

90
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:02,728
in October 2005: the slowest month in the
replay.

91
00:06:02,887 --> 00:06:07,767
Prices rose a little, then fell for years,
and even when he finally reached 80

92
00:06:07,817 --> 00:06:11,687
percent on paper, the index was still
below where it started.

93
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:16,247
He got there by paying the loan down, and
his own schedule,

94
00:06:16,274 --> 00:06:22,727
at his lower rate of 6.07 percent, reached
80 percent of the original price first,

95
00:06:22,754 --> 00:06:23,767
at payment 90.

96
00:06:23,907 --> 00:06:27,767
Whether a lender would have dropped his
insurance at that point,

97
00:06:27,815 --> 00:06:29,047
this data can't show.

98
00:06:30,034 --> 00:06:35,394
Same rule, same national index: what
differed was the month they started,

99
00:06:35,434 --> 00:06:37,074
and what prices did next.

100
00:06:37,421 --> 00:06:41,874
So a plan can be measured against two
benchmarks from this history.

101
00:06:42,074 --> 00:06:47,954
One is the schedule the law uses, which on
the example loan runs most of a decade.

102
00:06:48,134 --> 00:06:53,954
The other is history on paper: a plan that
counts on about two years matched the

103
00:06:54,004 --> 00:06:58,194
typical month, not the slow ones, and not
the lender's step.

104
00:06:58,567 --> 00:07:02,834
On the illustrative $400,000 home, the
other path,

105
00:07:02,868 --> 00:07:07,074
20 percent down, means $40,000 more up
front.

106
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:11,074
This video doesn't weigh that against
renting or waiting;

107
00:07:11,127 --> 00:07:13,954
it measures how long the insurance math
took.

108
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:18,567
How we know this, and what this video
leaves out,

109
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:20,727
is on this card and in the description.

110
00:07:25,734 --> 00:07:27,814
[thoughtful] So how long does the
insurance last?

111
00:07:28,134 --> 00:07:32,054
On the schedule, the answer is set the day
the loan starts;

112
00:07:32,134 --> 00:07:36,854
on paper, history gave answers from about
a year to more than 9 years,

113
00:07:36,914 --> 00:07:39,574
and on paper is never the same as removed.

114
00:07:39,686 --> 00:07:43,654
This is US only, and it's history, not a
forecast.

